###Pure
// ============================================================
// index-core -- index constituents and weights, aggregated by region
// ============================================================
// An index is a rule, not a portfolio: the rule says which instruments are in it and what
// fraction of the index each one is. The fractions are restruck on a REBALANCE -- a dated
// event with an announcement and an effective date -- so a constituent weight is only ever
// meaningful as of a rebalance, and that is why IdxConstituent hangs off IdxRebalance and
// not off IdxIndex.
//
// Two dependencies, and the project is the join between them:
//
//   core-instrument   a constituent IS an instrument. The weight belongs here; the ISIN,
//                     the issuer name and the asset class belong there, and are not copied.
//   core-geo          the issuer of that instrument sits in a country, and a country walks
//                     core-geo's own two-hop chain up to a coverage region.
//
// The aggregation -- IdxRegionWeight, IdxCountryWeight -- is the number a tracking fund
// actually looks at: not "which stocks are in the index" but "how much of the index is
// Japan", because that is what the fund has to hold and what a regional cap is written
// against.
//
// Package root index_core::, prefix IDX_ / Idx_ / idx per the contract.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ who publishes an index

// The benchmark administrator: MSCI, FTSE Russell, S&P Dow Jones, STOXX. Here because an
// index is only usable in a fund prospectus if its administrator is authorised, so the
// administrator is a fact about the index and not a label on it.
Class index_core::IdxProvider
{
   providerId: String[1];
   name: String[1];
   shortName: String[0..1];
   // ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 of the administrator's home regulator. A String and not a
   // core_geo::CgCountry, because a provider is administered in one place and the code is
   // the whole fact; the constituent side is where the geography actually gets navigated.
   homeCountryCode: String[0..1];
   isAuthorisedAdministrator: Boolean[1];
   methodologyUrl: String[0..1];
}

// A published range of indices sharing one methodology -- MSCI World, MSCI Emerging Markets
// and MSCI ACWI are one family. It exists so an eligibility rule can be stated once for the
// family rather than copied onto each index in it.
Class index_core::IdxIndexFamily
{
   familyId: String[1];
   name: String[1];
   assetClassScope: String[1];
   weightingPhilosophy: String[0..1];
   launchYear: Integer[0..1];
   isFlagship: Boolean[1];
}

// The index itself: a currency, a base date, a base level and a rule for restriking weights.
// The divisor lives on the daily level rather than here, because it changes on every
// corporate action and the index does not.
Class index_core::IdxIndex
{
   indexId: String[1];
   code: String[1];
   name: String[1];
   // The currency the level is published in. Free-float market cap indices are published in
   // several currencies off one set of weights, and this is the base one.
   currency: String[1];
   baseDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   baseLevel: Float[0..1];
   // FREE_FLOAT_MCAP, EQUAL, PRICE, FUNDAMENTAL. The single fact that decides what a
   // constituent weight means.
   weightingScheme: String[1];
   rebalanceFrequency: String[1];
   targetConstituentCount: Integer[0..1];
   isActive: Boolean[1];
}

// A rule an instrument must pass to be in the index at all -- minimum free float, minimum
// three-month traded value, a domicile restriction, a minimum listing history. Separate from
// the index because the rules are versioned: a threshold change is a new row with a new
// APPLIES_FROM, and the old rebalances stay explicable.
Class index_core::IdxEligibilityRule
{
   ruleId: String[1];
   ruleCode: String[1];
   description: String[1];
   thresholdValue: Float[0..1];
   thresholdUnit: String[0..1];
   appliesFrom: StrictDate[0..1];
   isHardScreen: Boolean[1];
}

// The cap that stops one name being the whole index. UCITS 5/10/40 is the reason this class
// exists: no constituent above 10%, and everything above 5% summing to no more than 40%, so
// a cap has both a single-name limit and a group limit and neither alone is the rule.
Class index_core::IdxWeightCap
{
   capId: String[1];
   capType: String[1];
   singleNameCapPct: Float[0..1];
   groupCapPct: Float[0..1];
   effectiveFrom: StrictDate[0..1];
   // 'UCITS', 'RIC', 'HOUSE' -- which rulebook the cap comes from, which decides whether it
   // can be waived.
   regulation: String[0..1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the dated event

// The review at which weights are restruck. The two dates are both load-bearing and are not
// the same date: the announcement is when the market learns the new list, the effective date
// is when a tracking fund must be holding it, and the drift between them is where index
// front-running happens.
Class index_core::IdxRebalance
{
   rebalanceId: String[1];
   announcementDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];
   // ANNUAL, SEMI_ANNUAL, QUARTERLY, AD_HOC -- an AD_HOC review is a deletion after a
   // takeover, not a scheduled one.
   reviewType: String[1];
   status: String[1];
   constituentCount: Integer[0..1];
   additionCount: Integer[0..1];
   deletionCount: Integer[0..1];
   // One-way turnover as a percent of index weight. What the fund pays to follow this
   // rebalance.
   turnoverPct: Float[0..1];

   // The invariant that makes weights weights: they sum to 100. Derived rather than
   // constrained, so a half-loaded rebalance is readable and reportable rather than
   // unopenable.
   totalWeightPct() { $this.constituents.weightPct->sum() }: Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the constituent

// One instrument's membership of one index as of one rebalance, and the weight it carries.
// This is the grain of the whole project: not (index, instrument) but
// (index, rebalance, instrument), because the same name at two reviews is two different
// weights and both are needed to explain the fund's turnover.
//
// The instrument itself is NOT redescribed here -- `instrument` navigates to
// core_instrument::Instrument for the ISIN, the name and the asset class. What is here is
// only what the index says: how much of it this name is, and how that number was arrived at.
Class index_core::IdxConstituent
{
   constituentId: String[1];
   // The published weight, after capping. Percent, so 4.2 means 4.2% of the index.
   weightPct: Float[1];
   // The weight the methodology produced before any cap was applied. Kept because the
   // difference between the two is the only evidence a cap actually bound.
   uncappedWeightPct: Float[0..1];
   sharesInIndex: Float[0..1];
   // The fraction of shares outstanding actually available to buy, 0.0 to 1.0. A
   // state-controlled issuer has a low one and is therefore a small index weight despite a
   // large market cap.
   freeFloatFactor: Float[0..1];
   cappingFactor: Float[0..1];
   isAddition: Boolean[1];
   isDeletion: Boolean[1];
   rankByWeight: Integer[0..1];
   // The vendor's region label as of this rebalance, and the column the region aggregation
   // groups on. A copy of geography, deliberately: an index file states the region a name
   // was classified in ON THE DAY, and reclassifying history when a country moves from
   // Emerging to Developed would make old rebalances irreproducible. The live truth is one
   // hop away through `issuerCountry`.
   regionCode: String[0..1];

   // Weight as a fraction rather than a percent, which is the form every tracking
   // calculation actually wants. Float because `/` widens even when both sides are Integer.
   weightFraction() { $this.weightPct / 100.0 }: Float[1];
   // Whether a cap bound this name at this rebalance.
   isCapped() { $this.cappingFactor->isNotEmpty() }: Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the aggregation

// Index weight by region, per rebalance. The class over the store's ~groupBy view: the
// engine folds the GROUP BY into the SQL, so a fund asking "how much of this index is EMEA"
// reads one row per region instead of eight hundred constituent rows.
//
// This is what a tracking fund actually looks at. A regional cap, a currency hedge ratio and
// a settlement-calendar headache are all decided off this number and none of them are
// decided off the constituent list.
//
// Note what a grouping does not produce: a region with no constituents at a rebalance forms
// no group and is simply absent, rather than appearing at zero weight.
Class index_core::IdxRegionWeight
{
   rebalanceId: String[1];
   regionCode: String[1];
   totalWeightPct: Float[0..1];
   constituentCount: Integer[0..1];
}

// The same rollup one level finer: index weight by issuer country. It exists separately
// because a regional cap and a country cap are different limits -- APAC at 30% says nothing
// about Japan at 25% -- and because this one joins straight onto core_geo::CgCountry, so a
// report can walk from a weight bucket up core-geo's chain to the coverage region without
// this project restating any geography.
Class index_core::IdxCountryWeight
{
   rebalanceId: String[1];
   issuerCountryCode: String[1];
   totalWeightPct: Float[0..1];
   constituentCount: Integer[0..1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ what the index does

// One published closing level for one index on one date. Composite grain (index, date).
// The three levels are three different products off one weight set: price return ignores
// dividends, gross return reinvests them whole, net return reinvests them after the
// withholding tax a foreign holder actually suffers -- which is why a fund's benchmark is
// almost always the net one.
Class index_core::IdxIndexLevel
{
   indexId: String[1];
   levelDate: StrictDate[1];
   priceLevel: Float[0..1];
   grossReturnLevel: Float[0..1];
   netReturnLevel: Float[0..1];
   // The number that absorbs corporate actions so the level does not jump on them.
   divisor: Float[0..1];
   marketCapUsd: Float[0..1];
}

// A fund whose mandate is to hold the index. The consumer of everything above: it is what
// makes the weights binding rather than descriptive, because a published weight change is an
// order this fund has to place.
Class index_core::IdxTrackingFund
{
   fundId: String[1];
   name: String[1];
   legalStructure: String[0..1];
   domicileCountryCode: String[0..1];
   aumUsd: Float[0..1];
   totalExpenseRatioBps: Float[0..1];
   // FULL, OPTIMISED, SYNTHETIC. Decides whether the fund's holdings can differ from the
   // constituent list at all.
   replicationMethod: String[1];
   trackingErrorBps: Float[0..1];
   inceptionDate: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// What the fund is actually holding on a date, against what the index says it should hold.
// The whole point is the difference: activeWeightBps is the fund's weight minus the index
// weight, and a full-replication fund whose active weight is not near zero has a problem.
Class index_core::IdxFundHolding
{
   holdingId: String[1];
   holdingDate: StrictDate[1];
   weightPct: Float[1];
   shares: Float[0..1];
   marketValueUsd: Float[0..1];
   // Fund weight minus index weight, in basis points. Signed.
   activeWeightBps: Float[0..1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ associations, internal
// The ends are the public surface of this project and are named to read at the call site.

// A family belongs to the administrator that publishes it.
Association index_core::IdxFamilyProvider
{
   provider: index_core::IdxProvider[1];
   families: index_core::IdxIndexFamily[*];
}

// An index belongs to a family, which is where its shared methodology comes from.
Association index_core::IdxIndexInFamily
{
   family: index_core::IdxIndexFamily[1];
   indices: index_core::IdxIndex[*];
}

// The review history of an index. Ordered by effectiveDate at the call site, not here.
Association index_core::IdxIndexRebalance
{
   index: index_core::IdxIndex[1];
   rebalances: index_core::IdxRebalance[*];
}

// The list as struck at one review. `constituents` is the property IdxRebalance's
// totalWeightPct() sums over.
Association index_core::IdxRebalanceConstituent
{
   rebalance: index_core::IdxRebalance[1];
   constituents: index_core::IdxConstituent[*];
}

// The screens the index applies. On the index rather than the family because a family
// tightens a threshold for one member and not the rest.
Association index_core::IdxIndexEligibility
{
   index: index_core::IdxIndex[1];
   eligibilityRules: index_core::IdxEligibilityRule[*];
}

// The caps in force for the index. [*] because UCITS and a house cap coexist and the
// binding one is the tighter.
Association index_core::IdxIndexCap
{
   index: index_core::IdxIndex[1];
   weightCaps: index_core::IdxWeightCap[*];
}

Association index_core::IdxIndexLevelHistory
{
   index: index_core::IdxIndex[1];
   levels: index_core::IdxIndexLevel[*];
}

// The rollup, reached from the review it rolls up. This is the navigation the theme is
// about: $rebalance.regionWeights->sortBy(w | $w.totalWeightPct).
Association index_core::IdxRebalanceRegionWeight
{
   rebalance: index_core::IdxRebalance[1];
   regionWeights: index_core::IdxRegionWeight[*];
}

Association index_core::IdxRebalanceCountryWeight
{
   rebalance: index_core::IdxRebalance[1];
   countryWeights: index_core::IdxCountryWeight[*];
}

// Which index a fund has undertaken to track. [1] on the index end: a fund without a
// benchmark is an active fund and is not this class.
Association index_core::IdxFundBenchmark
{
   index: index_core::IdxIndex[1];
   trackingFunds: index_core::IdxTrackingFund[*];
}

Association index_core::IdxFundHoldingLine
{
   fund: index_core::IdxTrackingFund[1];
   holdings: index_core::IdxFundHolding[*];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ associations, crossing
// The two boundaries this project exists to cross. Both are declared here rather than
// upstream, because neither dependency may know about indices: core-instrument is the
// instrument master and core-geo is a gazetteer, and a downward reference from either would
// be the dependency inversion this graph is built to detect.

// A constituent IS an instrument. [0..1] on the instrument end and not [1]: an index file
// arrives before the instrument master has the name set up, and an unmatched line is a
// mapping break to be worked -- not a row to be rejected. `indexMemberships` is added to
// core_instrument::Instrument, so an instrument can answer "which indices am I in, and at
// what weight" without core-instrument knowing indices exist.
Association index_core::IdxConstituentInstrument
{
   instrument: core_instrument::Instrument[0..1];
   indexMemberships: index_core::IdxConstituent[*];
}

// A constituent's issuer sits in a country -- the second crossing, and the one that makes
// the region aggregation checkable. From here core-geo's own chain does the rest:
//
//   $c.issuerCountry.subRegion.macroRegion.code
//
// which is the live coverage region, against `regionCode` which is what the vendor said on
// the day. When they disagree, the index has been reclassified and the fund's regional cap
// is about to bind differently.
Association index_core::IdxConstituentIssuerCountry
{
   issuerCountry: core_geo::CgCountry[0..1];
   issuedConstituents: index_core::IdxConstituent[*];
}

// The country rollup, hung directly off the country. `indexCountryWeights` on CgCountry is
// the end a fund reads to answer "how much index weight sits in this country", and it
// carries no geography of its own -- $w.country.subRegion.macroRegion is core-geo's chain.
Association index_core::IdxCountryWeightCountry
{
   country: core_geo::CgCountry[0..1];
   indexCountryWeights: index_core::IdxCountryWeight[*];
}

// A fund holding is a holding OF an instrument. The second crossing into core-instrument,
// and it is a different relationship from a constituent: a constituent is what the index
// says, a holding is what the fund did.
Association index_core::IdxFundHoldingInstrument
{
   instrument: core_instrument::Instrument[0..1];
   fundHoldings: index_core::IdxFundHolding[*];
}
